Claims identified & fact-check:

Claim 1: ADHD is essentially a "superpower" — defined as being able to concentrate only on things you enjoy, and not on things you don't enjoy
→ Unsupported/mischaracterized — this materially misrepresents ADHD. While ICD-11 does note that "inattention may not be evident when the individual is engaged in activities that provide intense stimulation and frequent rewards," this is one feature among many, not a definition of the disorder. Framing ADHD as an enjoyable-vs-boring dichotomy and a "superpower" omits the required cross-setting functional impairment, and overgeneralizes a partial truth into a false overall characterization

Claim 2: ADHD needs to be negatively impacting your quality of life for you to even receive a diagnosis
→ Supported — matches ICD-11's essential features (symptoms must have "a direct negative impact on academic, occupational or social functioning") and NICE 1.3.3, which requires "at least moderate psychological, social, or educational or occupational impairment" for diagnosis

Claim 3: A brain-imaging response-inhibition task shows a non-ADHD brain with substantial activation in relevant regions
→ Unsupported — this specific neuroimaging claim is not addressed anywhere in DSM-5-TR, ICD-11, or NICE, so it lacks support in the prescribed evidence base (regardless of its standing in the broader neuroscience literature not provided here)

Claim 4: Giving a non-ADHD brain methylphenidate produces some activation change on this same task
→ Unsupported — not addressed in the attached references

Claim 5: The same task performed by an ADHD brain shows markedly less activation in the relevant region
→ Unsupported — not addressed in the attached references

Claim 6: Giving stimulant medication to the ADHD brain produces a "significant increase" in activation
→ Unsupported — not addressed in the attached references

Claim 7: People with ADHD have different dopamine regulation and structurally different brains
→ Unsupported — the attached references describe ADHD in terms of behavioral/diagnostic criteria and note only general "soft" neurological abnormalities in sensory/motor performance; specific dopamine-regulation or structural-difference claims are not detailed in DSM-5-TR, ICD-11, or NICE as provided

Claim 8: ADHD is heritable
→ Supported — ICD-11 explicitly states ADHD "tends to run in families, with evidence of high heritability"

Claim 9: There are decades of peer-reviewed, high-quality research supporting ADHD's existence as a legitimate diagnosis
→ Supported (general) — consistent with the existence of well-established, evidence-reviewed diagnostic systems (ICD-11) and evidence-based clinical guidelines (NICE) built explicitly on systematic evidence reviews

